  "content": "```python\n\"\"\"\nBinary Search: Lower Bound Implementation\n\nThis module provides a lower_bound function that finds the first index where\na target value could be inserted into a sorted list while maintaining order.\nIt also demonstrates how to use this function for insertion into a sorted list.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom typing import List, TypeVar\n\n# Define a generic type variable for comparison\nT = TypeVar('T')\n\n\ndef lower_bound(arr: List[T], target: T) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Find the leftmost (first) position where `target` can be inserted into\n    the sorted list `arr` such that all elements to the left are strictly less\n    than `target`. If `target` is present in the list, returns the index of its\n    first occurrence. If not present, returns the index where it should be\n    inserted to maintain sorted order.\n\n    This is essentially finding the \"lower bound\" of the target in a sorted array.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    arr : List[T]\n        A sorted list (in ascending order) of comparable elements. The list must\n        already be sorted; this function does NOT sort it.\n    target : T\n        The value to search",
